o absolute, microscopic perfection to focus light correctly. If a glass lens deformed or sagged even a fraction of a millimeter over time, it would completely ruin the optics, rendering the instrument useless. Yet, the glass lenses used by astronomers like Galileo and Isaac Newton in the 1600s still focus light perfectly today. Despite gravity pulling on them for 400 years, they haven't warped at all. So, your logic is actually spot-on for how many materials behave in the real world! It just turns out that glass belongs to a specific category of rigid, elastic materials that will stubbornly hold their shape against gravity until the day they break.
